\viewkind4\uc1
\pard\qc\b\fs32 Entropy-Based Password Strength Analyzer\b0\fs24\par
\pard\qc Cybersecurity Project Documentation\par
\pard\sa200\par
\pard\b 1. Project Overview\b0\par
This project is a Python console application that estimates password strength using character categories, entropy, common-password patterns, and an estimated offline cracking time. The implementation is provided in password-strength-check.py.\par
\par
The project also includes a Windows executable named password-strength-check.exe, created with PyInstaller.\par
\par
\pard\b 2. Objectives\b0\par
\pard\li360 - Estimate password strength using measurable characteristics.\par
\pard\li360 - Identify common dictionary terms and simple patterns.\par
\pard\li360 - Provide a score, strength label, warnings, and recommendations.\par
\pard\li360 - Demonstrate basic password-security analysis for educational use.\par
\pard\li0\par
\pard\b 3. Main Features\b0\par
\pard\li360 - Detects lowercase letters, uppercase letters, digits, and special characters.\par
\pard\li360 - Calculates estimated entropy from password length and character pool size.\par
\pard\li360 - Checks for common terms such as password, 123456, qwerty, and admin.\par
\pard\li360 - Detects simple sequences such as abc and 123.\par
\pard\li360 - Detects three repeated characters such as aaa.\par
\pard\li360 - Reports a score from 0 to 100 and a strength label.\par
\pard\li360 - Provides crack-time estimates, warnings, and suggestions.\par
\pard\li0\par
\pard\b 4. Entropy Calculation\b0\par
The program first identifies which character categories appear in the password. The character pool is calculated as follows:\par
\pard\li360\f1 lowercase = 26, uppercase = 26, digits = 10, special characters = 32\f0\par
\par
Estimated entropy is calculated with:\par
\pard\li360\f1 entropy = password length * log2(character pool size)\f0\par
\par
The estimate is reduced by 15 bits for each matching common dictionary term, 10 bits for a detected sequence, and 10 bits for repeated characters.\par
\par
\pard\b 5. Crack-Time Estimate\b0\par
The program assumes an offline guessing speed of 10 billion guesses per second. It converts the estimated entropy into possible combinations and reports the result as instantaneous, minutes, hours, days, years, or centuries/practically uncrackable. This is a theoretical estimate, not a guarantee.\par
\par
\pard\b 6. Score Labels\b0\par
\pard\li360 - 80-100: VERY STRONG\par
\pard\li360 - 60-79: STRONG\par
\pard\li360 - 40-59: MODERATE\par
\pard\li360 - 20-39: WEAK\par
\pard\li360 - 0-19: VERY WEAK\par
\pard\li0\par
\pard\b 7. Requirements\b0\par
\pard\li360 - Python 3.9 or later for running the source code.\par
\pard\li360 - Windows for running the included executable.\par
\pard\li360 - No third-party package is required to run the Python source.\par
\pard\li0\par
\pard\b 8. How to Run the Python Source\b0\par
Open PowerShell in the Password strength Checker directory and run:\par
\pard\li360\f1 python password-strength-check.py\f0\par
\par
Enter a password when prompted. The program displays the strength report, entropy, estimated crack time, warnings, and recommendations.\par
\par
\pard\b 9. How to Run the Executable\b0\par
Double-click password-strength-check.exe, or run it from PowerShell:\par
\pard\li360\f1 .\\password-strength-check.exe\f0\par
\pard\li0\par
\pard\b 10. Limitations and Security Notice\b0\par
\pard\li360 - Entropy is an estimate based on character categories, not a measurement of how the password was created.\par
\pard\li360 - Crack time depends on the assumed guessing rate and attack method.\par
\pard\li360 - The blacklist is short and does not replace a breached-password database.\par
\pard\li360 - The tool should not be the only basis for password-security decisions.\par
\pard\li360 - Do not enter real passwords into software you do not trust.\par
\pard\li0\par
\pard\b 11. Project Files\b0\par
\pard\li360 - password-strength-check.py: Python source implementation.\par
\pard\li360 - password-strength-check.c++: Original C++ implementation.\par
\pard\li360 - password-strength-check.exe: Standalone Windows executable.\par
\pard\li360 - README.md: Quick-start instructions and limitations.\par
}
